    "howToCelebrate": "Easter Triduum is not a U.S. Holy Day of Obligation, yet Mass remains the most fitting centerpiece when your parish offers it. Let children hear one Gospel sentence at dinner the evening before Easter Triduum. Clearing schedules to participate in all three days. Clear nonessential commitments so Triduum liturgies can take priority — they cannot be replaced by private devotion alone. If illness or travel blocks church, pray the Mass texts from a trusted Catholic source, offer a decade of the Rosary, and make a spiritual communion — then return in person.",
    "holyDayAndMassObligation": "Easter Triduum is not a Holy Day of Obligation in the United States, yet it remains spiritually weighty inside Holy Week. Many Catholics still attend Mass, keep novenas, or honor local customs without canonical requirement. Confirm the exact date yearly through your parish or diocesan Ordo. Participating in Easter Triduum reinforces liturgical-year rhythm for children and adults alike.",
    "whyItMatters": "Walking day by day with Christ is how the Church inoculates against vague spirituality. One Liturgy, Three Days, One Mystery names gratitude when abundance makes prayer feel optional. Returning to Easter Triduum each cycle is formation, not redundancy: the mystery is stable; the believer is not.",
